# Thymeleaf boolean negation in `th:if` / `data-th-if`

XP Thymeleaf does **not** reliably evaluate JS-style `!` inside a **single** `${...}` compound expression. A condition like `${a and !b}` can treat the whole expression as truthy when `a` is set, so the negation never applies.

That causes **duplicate mutually exclusive branches** (e.g. both `useTarget` and non-`useTarget` anchors rendering).

## Forbidden

```html
<!-- BAD: ! inside one ${} does not negate -->
<a data-th-if="${obj.url and !obj.useTarget}" ...>

<!-- BAD: same pattern with other flags -->
<div data-th-if="${item.visible and !item.hidden}" ...>
```

## Required idioms

**Prefer** separate `${}` operands and Thymeleaf `not`:

```html
<a data-th-if="${obj.url} and ${not obj.useTarget}" ...>
```

**Or** keep branches mutually exclusive with `th:if` / `th:unless` (no compound negation):

```html
<a data-th-if="${obj.useTarget}" ...>
<a data-th-unless="${obj.useTarget}" ...>
```

Repo examples of the safe split-`${}` style: `analysis-full-view.html`, `shortcutblock.html`, `banner-medium.html`.

## Checklist when editing templates

1. Searching for `!` inside `th:if` / `data-th-if` — replace with `${not …}` or `th:unless`.
2. External vs internal link pairs — exactly one branch must render; verify with `useTarget: true` content.
3. Do not replace a working `th:unless` with a compound `${a and !b}` “cleanup”.
